The GetPRChecks function handles the "no checks configured" case by checking if the output contains "no checks", but this checks the wrong output variable. The function uses cmd.Output() which only captures stdout, but then checks string(output) in the error path where 'output' would be empty on error. The error message would be in stderr. Consider using cmd.CombinedOutput() instead, or check the error message directly using err.Error().
```suggestion
	output, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()
	if err != nil {
		// If no checks configured, return empty list
		if strings.Contains(string(output), "no checks") {
			return []PRCheck{}, nil
		}
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("failed to get PR checks: %w\n%s", err, output)
```

The RunCommit function restricts Claude's allowed tools to git commands only, but the --allowedTools flag format appears to use a custom pattern "Bash(git commit:*),Bash(git diff:*),Bash(git status:*)". This format should be verified against the actual claude CLI documentation, as incorrect tool specifications might cause the command to fail or behave unexpectedly. Consider adding a comment explaining this format or verifying it works as intended.
```suggestion
		"--dangerously-skip-permissions",
		// NOTE: The value for --allowedTools uses the Claude CLI's Bash tool syntax.
		// "Bash(<command pattern>:*)" restricts the on-device Bash tool to commands
		// matching the given pattern. Here we intentionally allow only a narrow set
		// of git commands needed for creating commits. Keep this in sync with the
		// Claude Code CLI documentation if the allowed-tools format changes.
```
